when the taliban took over kabul a  vancouver-based group canadian women for  afghan women quickly tried to get its 25  afghan employees and families out of the  country they'd been there promoting the  rights of women and girls but with last  summer's election just underway it  became difficult to reach canadian  immigration officials within days it was  clear that it wasn't business as usual  people were unavailable everything just  kind of froze for a couple of months  actually and that had real implications  for people's lives in fact documents  obtained by global news through several  access to information requests show a  government scrambling in the midst of an  election and overwhelmed by the flood of  requests for help ninety thousand emails  had piled up by mid-august and just one  in five phone calls placed by desperate  afghans to canadian officials was being  answered  five of the 25 families employed by  canadian women for afghan women did get  out but 20 families are still there and  it's why lauren oates was in tajikistan  on the afghan border trying to help i've  seen no progress at all on any of my  colleagues files  and it's getting  exceedingly dangerous for them i mean  our office has been visited by the  taliban  we've had many scary incidents and the  clock is ticking some are now worried  canada does not have the capacity to  handle a refugee crisis like the one in  ukraine and the still urgent crisis in  afghanistan  canada's immigration minister says it  does it's an enormous challenge we're  asking the system to do more than it's  ever been required to do at a time it's  already facing pressures uh but can we  do it i don't think we have a choice but  to do it one of the themes in thursday's  budget sources tell us is what they're  calling canada in the world and that's  taking the mean more defense spending  but advocates tell us they hope canada  in the world also means more spending on  refugees and foreign aid donna  david aiken in ottawa thank you
 

